skeptic.order_bias_note:
R1's flip means Jev has no stable view on R1 under this prompt. one_timeline goes from 0.385 to 0.745 just by moving from first to last real option. That is a 0.36 position effect, bigger than the 0.60 settling threshold's margin over chance. Self-reported confidence moved with it (0.36-0.41 vs 0.65-0.67), so confidence tracks position too and does not offset it. R2 also flips (recall_half_life 0.61 as listed, utility_exponent 0.495 reversed, with one reversed repeat unstable).

Three consequences for weighting Jev here. (a) The two-order protocol is weaker than it looks. With 3 real options, reversing keeps the middle option in the middle. owner_gate (R4) and journal_values (R1) sat in position 2 in both orders, so R4's 'settled' never had its position varied, and only R5's settlement actually survived a position swap. (b) jev_triage.py sends all five questions in one request with one shared state. The answers are not independent, and the state's framing ('held while a promoted generation has not yet been adopted by the owner') already assumes an owner adoption act, so the R4 result partly echoes its own prompt. (c) Two repeats of a near-deterministic call add little independent evidence.

Practical weighting: record Jev as a weak prior. On R1 it is uninformative, so the owner should rule from §5's argument and the V-TIMELINE check: one_timeline is the only option that makes T1-T3 and exact replay true. R4 and R5 can be cited as 'consistent with' the blueprint, not as independent support. Before the v7 paper relies on any settlement: rotate all orders (3 cyclic rotations, or all 6 permutations), send one question per request, use a neutral state that leaves out the blueprint's chosen wording, and treat any option whose mean moves by 0.2 or more across positions as contested. The sources are docs/v7/jev/summary.json and docs/v7/jev/requests.jsonl."}
